# Fork repair

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:charter_agreement_protocol, "~> 0.5.0"},
  {:charter_agreement_signer, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## What this notebook does

This notebook breaks a charter on purpose and then repairs it. Both parties accept two
different revisions that share the same revision number, so the charter forks. CAP reports
the fork as evidence and refuses to pick a side. The parties then accept a repair revision
that replaces both branches, and a single revision governs again.

Artifacts are signed with
[charter_agreement_signer](https://hex.pm/packages/charter_agreement_signer), as in the
[charter tour](charter-tour.livemd), with one exception. The fork needs a party that signs
something CAP's producer refuses to build, so one helper below signs those acceptances by
hand with `:crypto`. That is the only raw signing in the notebook, and it exists only to
play the misbehaving party.

The same flow runs as a script in `examples/supplier_fork_demo.exs` in the repository. Demo
keys only; never use them for anything real.

## Two parties and their descriptors

The same demo key handle as the tour: a module with `sign/2` and `key_identity/1` over an
in-memory Ed25519 key.

```elixir
alias CharterAgreementProtocol, as: CAP
alias CharterAgreementProtocol.{Acceptance, Canonicalization, Digest, Limits}

defmodule DemoKey do
  @behaviour CharterAgreementSigner

  def new(kid, seed_byte) do
    {public, private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, :binary.copy(<<seed_byte>>, 32))
    %{kid: kid, public: public, private: private}
  end

  @impl true
  def sign(message, key), do: {:ok, :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, message, [key.private, :ed25519])}

  @impl true
  def key_identity(key), do: {:ok, {key.kid, key.public}}
end

issuer_key = DemoKey.new("issuer-key", 1)
acceptor_key = DemoKey.new("acceptor-key", 2)

[issuer_key.kid, acceptor_key.kid]
```

Each party signs a descriptor for its key, and we keep its digest, which is the party's
identifier:

```elixir
publish = fn key ->
  claims = %{
    "protocol_revision" => 2,
    "descriptor_number" => 1,
    "verification_keys" => [
      %{
        "key_id" => key.kid,
        "algorithm" => "Ed25519",
        "public_key" => Base.url_encode64(key.public, padding: false),
        "status" => "active"
      }
    ],
    "attestation_hints" => [],
    "extensions" => %{"critical" => %{}, "optional" => %{}},
    "effective_from" => "2026-08-25T10:00:00Z"
  }

  {:ok, %{descriptor: compact}} = CharterAgreementSigner.sign_descriptor(claims, {DemoKey, key})
  {:ok, decoded} = CAP.decode_party_descriptor(compact, Limits.default())
  %{key: key, compact: compact, digest: CAP.descriptor_digest(decoded)}
end

issuer = Map.put(publish.(issuer_key), :role, "issuer")
acceptor = Map.put(publish.(acceptor_key), :role, "acceptor")
parties = [issuer, acceptor]
descriptors = [issuer.compact, acceptor.compact]

{:ok, issuer_chain} = CAP.verify_descriptor_chain([issuer.compact], Limits.default())
issuer_chain.topology
```

## Three revisions: a genesis and two competing successors

`revision` builds one charter revision with `encode_charter_revision/1` and reads back its
digest with `revision_digest/1`. Only the revision number, the legal text and the links to
earlier revisions change between calls.

```elixir
revision = fn number, legal_text, links ->
  claims =
    Map.merge(links, %{
      "protocol_revision" => 2,
      "revision_number" => number,
      "parties" => [
        %{"party_descriptor_digest" => issuer.digest, "role" => "issuer"},
        %{"party_descriptor_digest" => acceptor.digest, "role" => "acceptor"}
      ],
      "legal_text" => %{
        "content_digest" => :legal_text |> Digest.hash(legal_text) |> Digest.to_tagged(),
        "media_type" => "text/plain",
        "uri_hint" => "https://example.com/supplier-charter.txt"
      },
      "precedence_declaration" => "legal_text_governs",
      "attribution_declaration" => %{"basis" => "bound_deployments"},
      "termination_rules" => %{"reason_codes" => ["mutual", "breach"]},
      "abp_bindings" => [
        %{
          "party_role" => "issuer",
          "blueprint_id" => "example.demo/echo",
          "release_number" => 1,
          "content_digest" => "sha-256:b1Aw4cU5AbV9k8bdbZkRCsySDHGpTAwB-aQm57Wh7B8",
          "deployment_digest" => "sha-256:tWFr0caS0AWFJd2UcB9gZv3kNjIUP8xZ08WWM_h8xgo"
        }
      ],
      "receipt_profile" => "com.example.charter/default",
      "extensions" => %{"critical" => %{}, "optional" => %{}}
    })

  {:ok, bytes} = CAP.encode_charter_revision(claims)
  {:ok, decoded} = CAP.decode_charter_revision(bytes, Limits.default())
  digest = CAP.revision_digest(decoded)

  %{
    bytes: bytes,
    decoded: decoded,
    digest: digest,
    number: number,
    charter_id: Map.get(links, "charter_id", digest),
    prev: Map.get(links, "prev_revision_digest")
  }
end

genesis = revision.(1, "Supplier terms\n", %{"effective_from" => "2026-08-25T12:00:00Z"})

successor_of_genesis = %{
  "charter_id" => genesis.digest,
  "prev_revision_digest" => genesis.digest,
  "effective_from" => "2026-08-25T12:00:01Z"
}

usd = revision.(2, "Supplier terms, USD settlement\n", successor_of_genesis)
eur = revision.(2, "Supplier terms, EUR settlement\n", successor_of_genesis)

%{usd: usd.digest, eur: eur.digest}
```

The USD and EUR revisions have the same charter, the same predecessor and the same revision
number, but different terms, so their digests differ. A party may accept only one of them.

## Both parties accept genesis and USD

`acceptance_claims` builds the claims one party signs for one revision. `accept` has both
parties sign through the signer, against the view they hold:

```elixir
acceptance_claims = fn rev, party ->
  claims = %{
    "protocol_revision" => 2,
    "charter_id" => rev.charter_id,
    "revision_number" => rev.number,
    "revision_digest" => rev.digest,
    "party_descriptor_digest" => party.digest,
    "party_role" => party.role,
    "accepted_at" => "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"
  }

  if rev.prev, do: Map.put(claims, "prev_revision_digest", rev.prev), else: claims
end

accept = fn rev, view ->
  for party <- parties do
    {:ok, %{acceptance: compact}} =
      CharterAgreementSigner.sign_acceptance(
        acceptance_claims.(rev, party),
        {DemoKey, party.key},
        view
      )

    compact
  end
end

{:ok, view} = CAP.build_set([genesis.bytes], [], [], descriptors)
genesis_acceptances = accept.(genesis, view)

{:ok, view} = CAP.build_set([genesis.bytes, usd.bytes], genesis_acceptances, [], descriptors)
usd_acceptances = accept.(usd, view)

length(genesis_acceptances ++ usd_acceptances)
```

Four acceptances: both parties accepted genesis, then both accepted USD.

## The producer refuses to equivocate

Now the EUR revision arrives. The issuer's view already holds its own acceptance of USD at
revision number 2. Ask CAP's producer for the signing input of an EUR acceptance anyway, and
try the same through the signer:

```elixir
{:ok, view} =
  CAP.build_set(
    [genesis.bytes, usd.bytes, eur.bytes],
    genesis_acceptances ++ usd_acceptances,
    [],
    descriptors
  )

eur_claims = acceptance_claims.(eur, issuer)

%{
  producer:
    CAP.acceptance_signing_input(%{"kid" => issuer_key.kid, "claims" => eur_claims}, view),
  signer: CharterAgreementSigner.sign_acceptance(eur_claims, {DemoKey, issuer_key}, view)
}
```

Both refuse with `:signing_refused`, and the signer stops before it calls the key handle.
Accepting EUR would mean the same party signed two different revisions at the same number,
which is equivocation. The producer never builds that signature.

## A misbehaving party signs anyway

CAP cannot stop a party that skips the producer and signs the bytes itself. This helper does
exactly that: it builds the JWS header and payload by hand and signs with `:crypto`. It is
the only raw signing in this notebook, and it exists only to play the misbehaving party.

```elixir
sign_without_producer = fn claims, key ->
  header = %{"alg" => "Ed25519", "kid" => key.kid, "typ" => "cap+acceptance"}
  {:ok, header_bytes} = Canonicalization.encode_plain(header)
  {:ok, payload_bytes} = Canonicalization.encode_plain(claims)

  message =
    Base.url_encode64(header_bytes, padding: false) <>
      "." <> Base.url_encode64(payload_bytes, padding: false)

  signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, message, [key.private, :ed25519])
  message <> "." <> Base.url_encode64(signature, padding: false)
end

eur_acceptances =
  for party <- parties, do: sign_without_producer.(acceptance_claims.(eur, party), party.key)

length(eur_acceptances)
```

Both parties now hold signed acceptances of USD and of EUR. The header is the same
`Ed25519` header the producer would have written; the only thing wrong is what was signed.

## Each acceptance verifies on its own

Verify the issuer's USD and EUR acceptances one at a time. Facts structs print as
`<redacted>`, so read the fields you need:

```elixir
{:ok, usd_facts} =
  CAP.verify_acceptance(hd(usd_acceptances), usd.decoded, issuer_chain, Limits.default())

{:ok, eur_facts} =
  CAP.verify_acceptance(hd(eur_acceptances), eur.decoded, issuer_chain, Limits.default())

for facts <- [usd_facts, eur_facts] do
  Map.take(facts, [:party_role, :revision_number, :revision_digest, :signing_key_id])
end
```

Each acceptance is valid alone: the right key signed it, over a real revision. The problem
only shows when you hold both. `Acceptance.equivocation/2` turns the pair into evidence:

```elixir
{:ok, evidence} = Acceptance.equivocation(usd_facts, eur_facts)
Map.take(evidence, [:kind, :party_role, :revision_number, :revision_digests, :winner])
```

`winner: nil` is deliberate. CAP keeps both signed positions as evidence and never picks
one.

## The chain is contested

Verify everything the counterparty holds, then ask which revision governs once both
successors are in effect:

```elixir
fork_revisions = [genesis.bytes, usd.bytes, eur.bytes]
fork_acceptances = genesis_acceptances ++ usd_acceptances ++ eur_acceptances

{:ok, contested} =
  CAP.verify_chain(fork_revisions, fork_acceptances, descriptors, [], Limits.default())

%{
  chain_topology: contested.chain_topology,
  accepted_revision_digests: contested.accepted_revision_digests,
  fork_evidence: Enum.map(contested.fork_evidence, & &1.kind),
  governing: CAP.governing_revision(contested, ~U[2026-08-25 12:00:01Z])
}
```

The topology is `:forked`, and all three revisions count as accepted. `fork_evidence` holds
one `:sibling_revisions` entry for the two revisions at number 2, and one
`:equivocal_acceptances` entry for each party that signed both. Two accepted revisions
compete at that instant, so the governing answer is `{:ok, :contested}`. CAP does not break
the tie by digest order or by time. An application should not pick a branch on its own
while the view is contested.

## A receipt inside the fork

The issuer records an action under the USD revision while the fork is open. The signer
checks the receipt against the issuer's contested view, and the counterparty verifies it
against the same chain:

```elixir
receipt_claims = fn rev ->
  %{
    "protocol_revision" => 2,
    "charter_id" => genesis.digest,
    "revision_number" => rev.number,
    "revision_digest" => rev.digest,
    "issuing_party_role" => "issuer",
    "agent_party_role" => "issuer",
    "deployment_digest" => "sha-256:tWFr0caS0AWFJd2UcB9gZv3kNjIUP8xZ08WWM_h8xgo",
    "grant" => %{"scheme" => "host", "id" => "order-1042"},
    "invocation_id" => "123e4567-e89b-42d3-a456-426614174000",
    "decision" => "accepted",
    "outcome" => "effect_committed",
    "occurred_at" => "2026-08-25T12:00:01Z",
    "recorded_at" => "2026-08-25T12:00:02Z",
    "extensions" => %{"critical" => %{}, "optional" => %{}}
  }
end

{:ok, %{receipt: usd_receipt}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_receipt(receipt_claims.(usd), {DemoKey, issuer_key}, contested)

{:ok, receipt_facts} = CAP.verify_receipt(usd_receipt, contested, Limits.default())
Map.take(receipt_facts, [:governing_match, :chain_conflict, :outcome])
```

The receipt itself verifies. `governing_match` is `:undetermined` because no single
revision governs at `occurred_at`, so CAP cannot say whether USD was the right one.

`chain_conflict: :none` can look wrong inside a fork, but that field answers a narrower
question: does the receipt name a revision this verifier has never seen? The USD revision
is in the counterparty's chain, so the answer is no. The fork itself is reported by
`chain_topology` and `fork_evidence` above.

To see the other answer, give the counterparty a view that never saw EUR, and hand it a
receipt that names EUR:

```elixir
{:ok, %{receipt: eur_receipt}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_receipt(receipt_claims.(eur), {DemoKey, issuer_key}, contested)

{:ok, usd_only_view} =
  CAP.verify_chain(
    [genesis.bytes, usd.bytes],
    genesis_acceptances ++ usd_acceptances,
    descriptors,
    [],
    Limits.default()
  )

{:ok, unseen_facts} = CAP.verify_receipt(eur_receipt, usd_only_view, Limits.default())
Map.take(unseen_facts, [:governing_match, :chain_conflict])
```

Now `chain_conflict` is `:fork_evidenced`. The receipt names a revision at a number this
verifier already has an accepted revision for, so a fork exists somewhere outside this
view. `governing_match` is `:mismatch` because in this view USD governs, and the receipt
names EUR.

## The repair

The repair is a new revision both parties accept that covers every competing branch:
each one is either its ancestor through `prev_revision_digest` or listed in its
`supersedes`. Here it builds on USD and names both competing revisions in
`supersedes`, and the parties sign it through the signer against the full contested
view:

```elixir
repair =
  revision.(3, "Supplier terms, countersigned repair\n", %{
    "charter_id" => genesis.digest,
    "prev_revision_digest" => usd.digest,
    "supersedes" => Enum.sort([usd.digest, eur.digest]),
    "effective_from" => "2026-08-25T12:00:02Z"
  })

{:ok, view} =
  CAP.build_set(fork_revisions ++ [repair.bytes], fork_acceptances, [], descriptors)

repair_acceptances = accept.(repair, view)

{:ok, repaired} =
  CAP.verify_chain(
    fork_revisions ++ [repair.bytes],
    fork_acceptances ++ repair_acceptances,
    descriptors,
    [],
    Limits.default()
  )

{:ok, governing_after} = CAP.governing_revision(repaired, ~U[2026-08-25 12:00:02Z])

%{
  governing_after: governing_after,
  equals_repair: governing_after == repair.digest,
  superseded: repaired.superseded_revision_digests,
  fork_evidence_kept: Enum.map(repaired.fork_evidence, & &1.kind)
}
```

From the repair's `effective_from` on, the repair governs. The chain still holds the fork
evidence and both superseded revisions. Nothing is rewritten. The later revision takes
precedence.

## What just happened

* The producer and the signer refused to sign an equivocating acceptance.
* A party that signed anyway produced acceptances that verify one at a time, and CAP turned
  the pair into equivocation evidence with no winner.
* The chain reported `:contested`, and a receipt inside the fork verified with
  `governing_match: :undetermined`.
* A revision both parties accepted, superseding both branches, made one revision govern
  again.

CAP reported each of these as evidence. What to do about a fork is your application's call.
